teacher within the broader Sophistic movement of 5th century BCE Greece, advocating for the power of rhetoric."}, {"sourceSlug": "polus", "sourceName": "Polus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "ethical-philosophy", "targetName": "Ethical Philosophy", "context": "Polus's arguments, particularly on justice and power, indirectly influenced the development of ethical philosophy by providing a specific set of views for Socratic critique."}], "places": [{"name": "Acragas, Sicily", "role": "Origin City"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Setting of Depiction"}, {"name": "Ancient Greece", "role": "Cultural Context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Polus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "polus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q372798", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polus"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Polus holds significant historical importance as a representative figure of Sophistic thought, whose arguments regarding rhetoric and justice were immortalized and critiqued by Plato, thereby shaping foundational debates in Western philosophy. His depiction in *Gorgias* continues to inform discussions on power, morality, and the role of persuasion in society.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-19T12:25:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35442560106", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Polus was an ancient Greek philosophical figure best remembered for his depiction in the writing of Plato. He was a pupil of the famous orator Gorgias, and teacher of oratory from the city of Acragas, Sicily.", "newValue": "Polus, an ancient Greek philosophical figure from Acragas, Sicily, is primarily known not through his own extant writings, but as a compelling character in Plato's seminal dialogue, the *Gorgias*. Flourishing in the 5th century BCE, prior to his death around 460 BCE, Polus served as a prominent pupil of the celebrated orator Gorgias of Leontini and became a teacher of rhetoric himself. His significance lies in his articulate, albeit often caricatured, representation of certain Sophistic views on power, justice, and the utility of rhetoric, providing a critical intellectual foil for Socrates' ethical philosophy.

As a sophist, Polus advocated for rhetoric as a tool granting considerable power, suggesting that 'doing injustice, if one does not pay the penalty, is more profitable than suffering it.' In Plato's *Gorgias*, he engages in a heated debate with Socrates, vehemently defending the idea that tyrants and powerful individuals who commit injustice without consequences are the happiest. His arguments underscore the Sophistic emphasis on practical advantage and the manipulation of public opinion, contrasting sharply with Socratic ethics that prioritized the health of the soul and the pursuit of true justice over mere self-interest or unchecked power.

Polus's enduring legacy is thus inextricably linked to Plato's philosophical project, serving as a vivid historical example of the ethical and epistemological challenges posed by Sophistic thought in classical Athens. His dramatic portrayal solidified a particular understanding of rhetoric as potentially amoral and self-serving, influencing subsequent philosophical discourse on the relationship between power, justice, and the art of persuasion. Through Plato, Polus became a canonical figure in the philosophical exploration of the moral responsibilities inherent in public discourse and political life."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-19T12:25:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35442560106", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "500 BCE, Akragas"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "500 BCE, Akragas"}], "newValue": ["The emergence of the Sophistic movement in 5th century BCE Greece created a demand for teachers of rhetoric and public speaking.", "Gorgias of Leontini's widespread fame as an orator and rhetorician attracted numerous pupils, including Polus, who sought to master the art of persuasion.", "Plato's philosophical project to critique and define true knowledge and ethics often involved dramatizing contemporary intellectual figures like Polus as interlocutors for Socrates."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-19T12:25:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35442560106", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "460 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "460 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Polus's characterization in Plato's *Gorgias* served as a critical intellec